---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
const REPO = "https://github.com/VKSFY/zbroux-wiki";
---
<Base title="How the algorithms are made" description="Where the algorithms on this wiki come from, how moves are counted, how algorithms are ordered and how everything is checked.">
  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How the algorithms are made</h1>
    <p class="lede">The algorithm lists on this wiki were generated by computer search, not copied from other sheets. This page explains how they were found, how moves are counted, which algorithm is listed first, and how everything is checked.</p>

    <h2>How moves are counted</h2>
    <p>Every move count on this wiki is in <b>STM</b> (slice turn metric). Each turn of a face, a slice or a wide layer counts as one move, and half turns count as one too: M, M2, U2, r and R2 are each one move. The turn of the top layer before an algorithm (the pre-AUF) is not counted.</p>
    <p>In HTM, where a slice turn like M counts as two moves, the EODFDB algorithms average about 12.8 moves instead of 8.3.</p>

    <h2>Cases</h2>
    <p>Each case list is built by listing every position the step can start from, directly from the pieces, then grouping positions that only differ by a turn of the top layer into one case. That's how you know a list is complete. ZBLL comes out at 493 cases, the standard count, and the EODFDB count can be checked by hand with Burnside's lemma, as shown on the <a href="/methods/zbroux/eodfdb/#where-the-numbers-come-from">EODFDB page</a>.</p>

    <h2>EODFDB and EOStripe</h2>
    <p>These steps only use a few move types (M and U for EODFDB; R, r, M and U for EOStripe), so a breadth-first search can visit every position. Every algorithm listed is the shortest possible in STM using those moves.</p>
    <p>Shortest doesn't always mean nicest to turn. When a case has several shortest solutions, the order is:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><b>EODFDB:</b> fewest M turns (M' and M2 are preferred over M), then fewest half turns.</li>
      <li><b>EOStripe:</b> fewest M turns, then fewest r turns, then fewest half turns, because M is awkward one-handed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Nobody has hand-picked these yet, so look at the alternatives listed under each case too.</p>
    <p>For LEOR's EOStripe, the table of move counts matches the one RedstoneTim published from a different solver (ksolve++) exactly.</p>

    <h2 id="zbll">ZBLL</h2>
    <p><b>The ZBLL algorithms are unvetted.</b> They're correct, but no experienced ZBLL solver has chosen them yet, and the first algorithm for a case is often not the one most people use.</p>
    <p>They were found with a meet-in-the-middle search written for this wiki (<a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/tools/zbll_gen.mts`}>zbll_gen.mts</a>). It searches each of the move sets people use for speed (RU, RUD, RUF, RUL and RUr) separately, up to 16 moves, and keeps every solution it finds. Longer algorithms, or ones that mix more move types, aren't included. Each case lists up to four, ordered by this rule:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>each R or U turn costs 1, r costs 1.15, D, F and L cost 1.5, and B costs 2;</li>
      <li>half turns cost a little extra (0.1 for U2, 0.3 for R2, r2 and D2, 0.4 for F2, L2 and B2);</li>
      <li>a turn of the top layer before the algorithm costs 0.3;</li>
      <li>the lowest total comes first.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A rule like this can't feel finger tricks or regrips, which is why the list needs a human pass.</p>

    <h2>Checking</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Every algorithm is applied to its case on a simulated cube and must solve the step.</li>
      <li>EODFDB and EOStripe lists are checked a second time by a separately written simulator, including the mirror algorithms on the 46-case page.</li>
      <li>ZBLL is checked a second time with <a href="https://github.com/cubing/cubing.js">cubing.js</a>, the open-source library behind alg.cubing.net, which also confirms all 493 cases are different from each other.</li>
      <li>The "paste your scramble" lookups are tested against the same data with hundreds of random positions.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Data and source</h2>
    <p>All the case data is plain JSON, so you can check it with your own tools:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/eodfdb.json`}>eodfdb.json</a>: every EODFDB case, its algorithms and its mirror</li>
      <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/zbll.json`}>zbll.json</a>: every ZBLL case and its algorithms</li>
      <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/eostripe.json`}>eostripe.json</a>: EOStripe cases and the full move-count table</li>
      <li><a href={`${REPO}/blob/main/data/stats.json`}>stats.json</a>: the averages and counts quoted on this wiki</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The generators, solvers and checkers are in the <a href={`${REPO}/tree/main/tools`}>tools folder</a> of the <a href={REPO}>repository</a>. If you find a mistake, or know a better algorithm for a case, open an issue there.</p>
  </article>
</Base>
